COMMENT     On Mar 15, 2005 this sequence version replaced gi:88453.
            [FUNCTION] Hormone that plays a central role in whole body
            adaptation to stress (By similarity). Released by the hypothalamus
            primarily in response to physical or psychological stress and acts
            by binding to CRH receptor CRHR1, and to a lower extent, CRHR2
            (PubMed:18801728, PubMed:3003585, PubMed:32004469). CRH-dependent
            signaling is a primary mediator of the neuroendocrine, autonomic
            (fight-or-flight) and behavioral responses to stress, acting as a
            key regulator of adaptation by activating the
            hypothalamus-pituitary-adrenal axis, leading to corticotropin
            hormone (ACTH) production (By similarity). Acts as a 'placental
            clock' during pregnancy, regulating gestational length, fetal
            development and the timing of labor (PubMed:7585095). Also acts as
            a regulator of blastocyst implantation by promoting
            decidualization, regulating trophoblast invasion and facilitating
            maternal immune tolerance (PubMed:11590404).
            {ECO:0000250|UniProtKB:Q8CIT0, ECO:0000269|PubMed:11590404,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:18801728, ECO:0000269|PubMed:3003585,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:32004469, ECO:0000269|PubMed:7585095}.
            ACTIVITY REGULATION: Specifically inhibited by CRHBP, which
            prevents binding to CRH receptors CRHR1 and CRHR2.
            {ECO:0000269|PubMed:18234674, ECO:0000269|PubMed:1846945}.
            [INTERACTION] P06850; P34998: CRHR1; NbExp=5; IntAct=EBI-3870390,
            EBI-3870393; P06850; Q13324-1: CRHR2; NbExp=4; IntAct=EBI-3870390,
            EBI-26585317; P06850; P48730-2: CSNK1D; NbExp=3;
            IntAct=EBI-3870390, EBI-9087876; P06850; A8MQ03: CYSRT1; NbExp=3;
            IntAct=EBI-3870390, EBI-3867333; P06850; Q15323: KRT31; NbExp=3;
            IntAct=EBI-3870390, EBI-948001; P06850; Q8IUG1: KRTAP1-3; NbExp=3;
            IntAct=EBI-3870390, EBI-11749135; P06850; Q9BYS1: KRTAP1-5;
            NbExp=3; IntAct=EBI-3870390, EBI-11741292; P06850; P60410:
            KRTAP10-8; NbExp=3; IntAct=EBI-3870390, EBI-10171774; P06850;
            P26371: KRTAP5-9; NbExp=3; IntAct=EBI-3870390, EBI-3958099; P06850;
            Q9BYQ3: KRTAP9-3; NbExp=3; IntAct=EBI-3870390, EBI-1043191; P06850;
            Q99750: MDFI; NbExp=3; IntAct=EBI-3870390, EBI-724076; P06850;
            P0DPK4: NOTCH2NLC; NbExp=3; IntAct=EBI-3870390, EBI-22310682;
            P06850; Q8IWV7: UBR1; NbExp=2; IntAct=EBI-3870390, EBI-711736.
            [SUBCELLULAR LOCATION] Secreted {ECO:0000269|PubMed:18559919,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:2839838}.
            [TISSUE SPECIFICITY] Produced by the hypothalamus and placenta.
            {ECO:0000269|PubMed:2783917, ECO:0000269|PubMed:3262120}.
            [INDUCTION] In response to physical or psychological stress
            (Probable). By fetal adrenal glucocorticoids (PubMed:2839838).
            {ECO:0000269|PubMed:2839838, ECO:0000305}.
            [PTM] The precursor protein is cleaved into corticotropin-releasing
            hormone (CRH) by neuroendocrine convertases PCSK2 (PubMed:8879041).
            It is then amidated at the C-terminus, completing its activation
            (Probable). {ECO:0000269|PubMed:8879041,
            ECO:0000305|PubMed:6605851}.
            [MISCELLANEOUS] Corticotropin-releasing hormone levels are strongly
            reduced in the cerebral cortex of Alzheimer's disease patients.
            {ECO:0000269|PubMed:3003585}.
            [SIMILARITY] Belongs to the sauvagine/corticotropin-releasing
            factor/urotensin I family. {ECO:0000305}.
            [WEB RESOURCE] Name=Wikipedia; Note=Corticotropin-releasing hormone
            entry;
            URL='https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corticotropin-
            releasing_hormone'.
