COMMENT     On Nov 25, 2009 this sequence version replaced gi:74721711.
            [FUNCTION] Guanine nucleotide-binding proteins (G proteins) are
            involved as a modulator or transducer in various transmembrane
            signaling systems (PubMed:29925951, PubMed:33762731,
            PubMed:34239069, PubMed:35610220, PubMed:35714614, PubMed:35835867,
            PubMed:36087581, PubMed:36989299, PubMed:37327704, PubMed:37935376,
            PubMed:37935377, PubMed:37963465, PubMed:38168118, PubMed:38552625,
            PubMed:39103320). The beta and gamma chains are required for the
            GTPase activity, for replacement of GDP by GTP, and for G
            protein-effector interaction (PubMed:29925951, PubMed:33762731,
            PubMed:34239069, PubMed:35610220, PubMed:35714614, PubMed:35835867,
            PubMed:36087581, PubMed:36989299, PubMed:37327704, PubMed:37935376,
            PubMed:37935377, PubMed:37963465, PubMed:38168118, PubMed:38552625,
            PubMed:39103320). Involved in bitter and sweet taste transduction
            as well as in umami (monosodium glutamate, monopotassium glutamate,
            and inosine monophosphate) taste transduction (PubMed:40339580,
            PubMed:38600377, PubMed:38776963). {ECO:0000269|PubMed:29925951,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:33762731, ECO:0000269|PubMed:34239069,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:35610220, ECO:0000269|PubMed:35714614,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:35835867, ECO:0000269|PubMed:36087581,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:36989299, ECO:0000269|PubMed:37327704,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:37935376, ECO:0000269|PubMed:37935377,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:37963465, ECO:0000269|PubMed:38168118,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:38552625, ECO:0000269|PubMed:38600377,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:38776963, ECO:0000269|PubMed:39103320,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:40339580}.
            [SUBUNIT] G proteins are composed of 3 units, alpha, beta and gamma
            (PubMed:19713215, PubMed:28219978, PubMed:29925951,
            PubMed:33762731, PubMed:34239069, PubMed:35610220, PubMed:35714614,
            PubMed:35835867, PubMed:36087581, PubMed:36989299, PubMed:37327704,
            PubMed:37935376, PubMed:37935377, PubMed:37963465, PubMed:38168118,
            PubMed:38552625, PubMed:39103320). In this context, interacts with
            GNB2 (PubMed:28219978). The heterodimer formed by GNB1 and GNG2
            interacts with ARHGEF5 (PubMed:19713215). The heterodimer formed by
            GNB1 and GNG2 interacts with GRK2 (By similarity). The heterodimer
            formed by GNB1 and GNG2 interacts with the ghrelin receptor GHSR
            (PubMed:35027551, PubMed:39833471). Forms a complex with GNAO1 and
            GNB1; this complex associates with Mu-type opioid receptor OPRM1,
            D(2) dopamine receptor DRD2, neurotensin receptor type 1 NTSR1 and
            beta-2 adrenergic receptor ADRB2 (PubMed:39103320). Forms complexes
            with TAS2R14 and G proteins; these complexes play a role in the
            perception of bitterness (PubMed:38600377, PubMed:38776963).
            Component of the TAS2R14-GNAI1 complex, consisting of TAS2R14,
            GNAI1, GNB1 and GNG2 (PubMed:38600377, PubMed:38776963). Component
            of the TAS2R14-GNAT3 complex, consisting of TAS2R14, GNAT3, GNB1
            and GNG2 (PubMed:38600377, PubMed:38776963). Component of the
            TAS2R14-GNAS2 complex, consisting of TAS2R14, GNAS2, GNB1 and GNG2
            (PubMed:38776963). Component of the sweet taste receptor complex,
            consisting of TAS1R2, TAS1R3, GNAT3, GNB1 and GNG2; this complex
            mediates sweet taste transduction (PubMed:40339580).
            {ECO:0000250|UniProtKB:P63212, ECO:0000269|PubMed:19713215,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:28219978, ECO:0000269|PubMed:29925951,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:33762731, ECO:0000269|PubMed:34239069,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:35027551, ECO:0000269|PubMed:35610220,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:35714614, ECO:0000269|PubMed:35835867,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:36087581, ECO:0000269|PubMed:36989299,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:37327704, ECO:0000269|PubMed:37935376,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:37935377, ECO:0000269|PubMed:37963465,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:38168118, ECO:0000269|PubMed:38552625,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:38600377, ECO:0000269|PubMed:38776963,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:39103320, ECO:0000269|PubMed:39833471,
            ECO:0000269|PubMed:40339580}.
            [INTERACTION] P59768; P62873: GNB1; NbExp=14; IntAct=EBI-602681,
            EBI-357130.
            [SUBCELLULAR LOCATION] Cell membrane {ECO:0000305}; Lipid-anchor
            {ECO:0000305}; Cytoplasmic side {ECO:0000305}.
            [TISSUE SPECIFICITY] Expressed in fetal tissues, including testis,
            adrenal gland, brain, white blood cells and brain.
            {ECO:0000269|PubMed:10833460}.
            [SIMILARITY] Belongs to the G protein gamma family. {ECO:0000305}.
